WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A new vest design could cut cancer risk for astronauts heading beyond Earth's protective field. This is fascinating Frontiers, episode 173. The vest uses layered materials to block high-energy particles that penetrate spacecraft walls during long missions. Researchers tested prototypes showing measurable dose reduction for crews traveling to Mars or beyond the magnetosphere. The design targets galactic cosmic rays that current whole-shielding leaves unaddressed. Follow-up trials will

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:54.940
measure performance in simulated deep-space radiation environments before any flight hardware decisions. Engineers focused on materials that remain lightweight while providing consistent protection across different particle energies. The vest is intended as a wearable supplement rather than a replacement for spacecraft shielding. Data from the prototype tests indicate lower absorbed doses in key-body areas during exposure periods.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:01:18.340
Now shifting to conditions on Earth itself. Landsat satellite images spanning decades reveal how breeding colonies shift when fast ice breaks up earlier each season. Scientists track colony locations and sizes to map which sites remain stable and which are abandoned. The data helps separate natural variability from longer-term ice-loss trends around Antarctica.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:45.660
Continued monitoring will show whether colonies can relocate fast enough to match the changing coastline. Images captured over multiple decades allow comparison of colony positions relative to the ice edge at consistent times of year. Researchers examined both the timing of ice breakup and the distance colonies must travel to reach stable breeding platforms. The analysis distinguishes short-term fluctuations from sustained changes in ice duration.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:02:09.160
On a different note, new models are revealing how entire galaxies keep their centers in balance over billions of years. A team at the Leibniz Institute for Astrophysics Potsdam ran high-resolution galaxy models that couple central black holes with their surrounding stellar disks. The runs show gas inflows and stellar feedback keep the central regions growing in step over billions of years.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:28.460
The models reproduce observed Milky Way properties such as the boxy bulge and nuclear star cluster. Next steps include comparing the simulated stellar populations with upcoming spectroscopic surveys. The simulations incorporate both gravitational dynamics and gas cooling processes at high spatial resolution.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:46.920
Results indicate that feedback from the central black hole regulates the rate of new star formation in the inner galaxy. The models stellar orbits match the observed boxy shape of the Milky Way bulge. Understanding how galaxies grow pairs naturally with the infrastructure that lets us study them from afar.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:03:01.300
The new dish at the Goldstone Complex adds capacity to the 34 meter subnet used for tracking missions across the solar system. It joins three existing antennas and will support more than 40 active spacecraft once commissioned.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:13.040
Engineers completed integration and began initial pointing pests this month. The addition helps maintain continuous coverage as multiple Mars and outer planet missions operate simultaneously.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:21.680
The antenna increases the network's ability to receive weak signals from distant probes without extending existing observation schedules.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:35.820
Commissioning activities include calibration of the radio frequency systems and verification of tracking accuracy. Once operational, the new dish will share the load during periods when several spacecraft require simultaneous contact.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:51.860
More listening power on the ground comes at a time when we are learning how quickly planet forming material can disappear. Observations of several young stars reveal that the gas reservoir needed for giant planet growth disperses on time scales shorter than previously modeled.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:04:03.520
The telescope measured ultraviolet and infrared signatures of photo evaporation driven by the central star's radiation. This process appears stronger in disks around higher mass stars,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:13.300
altering the window available for planet assembly. Follow-up programs will target additional disks to test how stellar type and disk mass affect the dispersal rate.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:23.580
The measurements capture the rate at which gases lifted away from the disk surface by stellar ultraviolet light. Discs around more massive stars show faster gas loss,

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:33.940
narrowing the time available for cores to accrete thick atmospheres. The findings adjust earlier estimates of how long the gas phase lasts in typical planet forming environments.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:43.180
JWST observations of dispersing planet forming disks show that the gas giant formation window can close in just a few million years.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:54.480
The telescope captured direct tracers of ultraviolet driven winds leaving the disk surface, confirming that stellar radiation not just internal turbulence removes the raw material.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:07.200
This tightens the timeline models must satisfy and raises the question of how often massive planets still manage to form. What fraction of disks lose their gas before cores can accrete thick envelopes?

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:21.200
Picture a disk of gas and dust around a young star the size of our entire solar system. Now shrink that disk until its outer edge sits inside the orbit of Jupiter, and you have the typical scale where gas giants must finish forming.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:33.240
Inside that compact region, the central star's ultraviolet light heats the disk surface to thousands of degrees, launching a wind that carries away hundreds of earth masses of material each million years.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:43.780
The process is fastest near the star, so the inner disk empties first while the outer region's linger, creating a shrinking reservoir that planets must tap before it vanishes.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:55.620
One surprising measurement from recent observations shows some disks lose half their gas in under 3 million years, a time scale shorter than the orbital period of Neptune around our own sun.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:03.600
Yet we still do not know why a few disks retain gas far longer than their neighbors, even when the stars appear nearly identical.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:13.140
The unanswered question is whether an unseen companion or a different magnetic field geometry can suppress the wind and buy extra time for planet growth.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:21.820
Models must now account for the possibility that only a narrow subset of disks survive long enough for giant planets to assemble their envelopes.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:32.820
Observations continue to reveal that the dispersal mechanism operates across a range of stellar masses, yet the exact efficiency remains difficult to predict from star properties alone.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:42.540
Future data sets will test whether external factors such as nearby massive stars can accelerate or slow the loss of gas in otherwise similar systems.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:50.760
With planet formation timelines tightening, it is worth noting how future explorers might one day recycle what they already have on the moon.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:58.260
The agency will name the final phase winners of its lunar recycle challenge on August 28th at the University of Alabama.

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:05.980
The competition asks teams to design systems that turn non-metabolic waste into usable resources for future lunar bases.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.820
Finalists have been testing prototypes that process plastics, metals, and other station trash.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:16.720
The results will inform technology road maps for sustained surface operations.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:23.180
The event will take place at the University of Alabama's Lee Styslinger College of Engineering in Tuscaloosa.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:29.220
Teams developed hardware capable of handling waste streams that do not include crew-metabolic output.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:35.820
Prototype evaluations focused on conversion efficiency and reliability under simulated lunar conditions.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:42.540
While hardware challenges continue, a new academic program is preparing the next generation to tackle them.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:51.840
Several colleges are combining courses in engineering, planetary science, and policy into a single minor available starting next academic year.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:58.540
Students will complete a capstone project that can involve mission design, data analysis, or science communication.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:07.520
Faculty from multiple departments develop the curriculum to prepare graduates for roles at NASA, commercial space firms, and research institutes.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.160
Enrollment opens this fall for the 2026-2027 catalog.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:19.900
The program draws on existing courses across engineering, science, and social science departments to create an integrated pathway.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:28.400
Capstone options allow students to choose between hardware design projects, analysis of mission data sets, or public outreach activities.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:35.180
The curriculum was shaped by faculty input to align with workforce needs at government agencies and private companies.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.160
Registration for the first cohort begins during the upcoming fall enrollment period.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:48.400
Before we go, keep an eye on follow-up JWST targets that will test how stellar type and disk mass affect gas dispersal rates.

